  "u5-ns3": passage("u5-ns3", "Machines, mills, and coal in Britain",
    "In the middle 1700s, most British cloth was spun and woven by hand in rural homes, often by families working for merchants who supplied raw materials and bought the finished goods. A series of inventions changed cotton spinning. James Hargreaves's spinning jenny (about 1764) let one worker spin several threads at once. Richard Arkwright's water frame (1769) used water power to spin strong thread, and in 1771 Arkwright built a mill at Cromford that brought hundreds of workers under one roof and set their hours by the machines. Samuel Crompton's spinning mule (1779) combined features of both. Later improvements to the steam engine, especially James Watt's design of the 1770s, let mills run on coal instead of falling water, so factories could be built in towns near coalfields and ports. Britain had large coal deposits close to the surface, and coal also fueled new ways to make iron. Cotton imports grew rapidly, much of the cotton coming from plantations in the southern United States worked by enslaved people."),
  "u5-ns4": {
    id: "u5-ns4", name: "Machine industry beyond Britain", intro: "Use the map below to answer the questions that follow.", label: "Map",
    sources: [{ title: "Map", blocks: map("u5r-industry-spreads", "Machine industry beyond Britain: some early mills and works, with the year each began", "Wikipedia's articles on John Cockerill, John Hughes, Krupp, the Merrimack Manufacturing Company, Francis Cabot Lowell, the Edgar Thomson Steel Works, the cotton mill (for Bombay), and Toyobo (for the Osaka Spinning Company), and Encyclopaedia Britannica, \"Seraing\"") }],
  },
  "u5-ns5": passage("u5-ns5", "Wires, steam, and cold storage",
    "New technologies of the 1800s made the world smaller. Samuel Morse sent the first long-distance telegraph message in the United States in 1844, and telegraph lines soon ran along railways in many countries. A lasting cable across the Atlantic began working in 1866, and by the early 1870s cables linked Britain to India and Australia, so news and orders that once took months took hours. Steamships, at first used on rivers, grew larger and more efficient with iron and then steel hulls, screw propellers, and better engines, and they began to replace sailing ships on long routes after the Suez Canal opened in 1869. In 1882 the ship Dunedin carried a cargo of frozen meat from New Zealand to Britain, and refrigerated ships soon brought meat from Argentina, Australia, and New Zealand to European markets."),
